Female genital mutilation (FGM) can cause both immediate and long-term health problems. The risks vary depending on the type of cutting, the circumstances in which it was carried out, and whether complications developed.

Possible health risks include:

severe pain, bleeding, shock, infection, and difficulty passing urine;
injury to nearby genital or urinary structures;
scar tissue, cysts, and persistent pelvic or genital pain;
recurrent urinary or vaginal infections;
painful menstruation or difficulty with menstrual flow;
pain during sex and other sexual-health difficulties;
complications during pregnancy and childbirth, including obstructed labour, tearing, heavy bleeding, and increased risk to the baby;
psychological effects such as anxiety, traumatic stress, depression, or distress linked to the experience.

Someone affected by FGM can seek support from a doctor, nurse, sexual-health clinic, maternity service, gynaecology service, mental-health professional, or specialist FGM service. These services can assess physical complications, provide treatment, discuss sexual and reproductive health, offer psychological support, and refer for specialist procedures where appropriate.

If there is heavy bleeding, severe pain, fever, difficulty urinating, loss of consciousness, or another serious complication, urgent medical care is needed.

If a child or young person is at risk of being subjected to FGM, or someone is being pressured or threatened, this should be treated as a safeguarding concern and reported promptly to appropriate health, social-care, child-protection, or emergency services.

Female genital mutilation, FGM, can cause immediate and long-term health problems.

Possible risks include:

severe pain and heavy bleeding;
infection;
difficulty passing urine;
injury to surrounding genital tissue;
scar tissue, cysts, and long-term pelvic or genital pain;
repeated urinary or vaginal infections;
painful menstruation;
pain during sex and other sexual-health problems;
complications during pregnancy and childbirth, including difficult labour, tearing, and heavy bleeding;
psychological effects such as anxiety, traumatic stress, depression, or emotional distress.

A person affected by FGM can seek support from a doctor, nurse, sexual-health clinic, gynaecology service, maternity service, mental-health professional, or specialist FGM service where available. These services can assess complications, provide treatment, support sexual and reproductive health, and offer psychological care.

Urgent medical help is needed if there is heavy bleeding, severe pain, fever, difficulty urinating, fainting, or another serious complication.

If a child or young person is at risk of FGM, or someone is being pressured or threatened to undergo it, this should be treated as a safeguarding concern and reported promptly to appropriate child-protection, healthcare, social-care, police, or emergency services.

