Dídá ojú abẹ́ obìnrin, tí a tún ń pè ní FGM, lè fa ìṣòro ìlera lẹ́sẹ̀kẹsẹ àti ní ọjọ́ iwájú.

Àwọn ewu tó lè ṣẹlẹ̀ ni:

ìrora tó le gan-an àti ẹ̀jẹ̀ púpọ̀;
àkóràn;
ìṣòro nígbà tí ẹni bá fẹ́ tọ̀;
ìfarapa sí àwọn apá míì tó wà lẹ́gbẹ̀ẹ́;
àpá líle, cyst, tàbí ìrora tó máa ń bá ẹni lọ;
àkóràn tó ń tún padà ní ọ̀nà ìtọ̀ tàbí abẹ́;
ìrora nígbà oṣù;
ìrora nígbà ìbálòpọ̀;
ìṣòro nígbà oyún àti ìbímọ, bíi ìbímọ tó nira tàbí ẹ̀jẹ̀ púpọ̀;
ìṣòro ọpọlọ àti ẹ̀dá ọkàn, bíi ìbànújẹ́, ìbẹ̀rù, anxiety, tàbí traumatic stress.

Ẹni tí FGM ti kan lè lọ sí ilé ìwòsàn, ọ̀dọ̀ dókítà tàbí nọọsi, sexual-health clinic, maternity service, gynaecology service, mental-health professional, tàbí specialist FGM service. Wọ́n lè ṣàyẹ̀wò ìṣòro tó wà, fúnni ní ìtọ́jú, àti pèsè ìtìlẹ́yìn nípa ìlera ibímọ àti ọpọlọ.

Bí ẹni náà bá ní ẹ̀jẹ̀ púpọ̀, ìrora tó le, ibà, ìṣòro láti tọ̀, tàbí ó ń dákú, ó yẹ kí ó gba ìtọ́jú pajawiri lẹ́sẹ̀kẹsẹ.

Bí ọmọ tàbí ọdọ kan bá wà nínú ewu FGM, tàbí ẹni kan bá ń fi ipa mú un láti ṣe é, ó yẹ kí a kàn sí ilé ìwòsàn, iṣẹ́ safeguarding, child-protection, social-care, tàbí emergency services kíákíá.

Dídá ojú abẹ́ obìnrin, FGM, lè fa ìṣòro ìlera lẹ́sẹ̀kẹsẹ̀ àti ní ọjọ́ iwájú.

Àwọn ewu tó lè wáyé ni:

ìrora tó le àti ẹ̀jẹ̀ púpọ̀;
àkóràn;
ìṣòro láti tọ̀;
ìfarapa sí àwọn àsopọ̀ ara tó wà nítòsí;
àpá líle, cyst, tàbí ìrora tó máa ń pé;
àkóràn ito tàbí abẹ́ tó ń tún padà;
ìrora nígbà oṣù;
ìrora nígbà ìbálòpọ̀ àti àwọn ìṣòro ìlera ìbálòpọ̀ míì;
ìṣòro nígbà oyún àti ìbímọ́, bí ìbímọ́ tó nira tàbí ẹ̀jẹ̀ púpọ̀;
ìṣòro ọpọlọ bí ìbẹ̀rù, anxiety, traumatic stress, tàbí ìbànújẹ́.

Ẹni tí FGM ti kan lè wá ìrànlọ́wọ́ lọ́wọ́ dókítà, nọọsi, sexual-health clinic, gynaecology service, maternity service, mental-health professional, tàbí specialist FGM service níbi tí wọ́n bá wà. Wọ́n lè ṣàyẹ̀wò àwọn ìṣòro, fúnni ní ìtọ́jú, àti pèsè ìtìlẹ́yìn fún ìlera ìbálòpọ̀, ìbímọ́, àti ọpọlọ.

Bí ẹni náà bá ní ẹ̀jẹ̀ púpọ̀, ìrora tó le, ibà, ìṣòro láti tọ̀, dídákú, tàbí ìṣòro tó le míì, ó yẹ kí ó gba ìtọ́jú pajawiri lẹ́sẹ̀kẹsẹ̀.

Bí ọmọbìnrin kan bá wà nínú ewu FGM, tàbí ẹnikan bá ń fi ipa tàbí ìhalẹ̀ mú un láti ṣe é, ó yẹ kí a kàn sí iṣẹ́ safeguarding, child-protection, social-care, ọlọ́pàá, tàbí iṣẹ́ pajawiri tó yẹ kíákíá.

Ibi nwaanyị ugwu, nke a makwaara dị ka female genital mutilation (FGM), nwere ike ibute nsogbu ahụike ozugbo ma ọ bụ mgbe ọtụtụ afọ gachara.

Nsogbu ndị a na-ahụkarị gụnyere:

nnukwu mgbu na ọbara ọgbụgba;
ọrịa nje;
nsogbu ịpụ mmamịrị;
mmerụ ahụ n’akụkụ anụ ahụ ndị ọzọ dị nso;
ọnya siri ike, cyst, ma ọ bụ mgbu na-adịte aka;
ọrịa mmamịrị ma ọ bụ ọrịa n’akụkụ ikpu na-alọghachi ugboro ugboro;
mgbu n’oge ịhụ nsọ;
mgbu ma ọ bụ nsogbu n’oge mmekọahụ;
nsogbu n’oge ime na ịmụ nwa, dịka ịmụ nwa siri ike ma ọ bụ nnukwu ọbara ọgbụgba;
nsogbu uche dịka nchekasị, egwu, mwute, ma ọ bụ traumatic stress.

Onye FGM metụtara nwere ike ịchọ enyemaka n’aka dọkịta, nọọsụ, ụlọ ọgwụ, sexual-health clinic, maternity service, gynaecology service, mental-health professional, ma ọ bụ specialist FGM service. Ha nwere ike nyochaa nsogbu anụ ahụ, nye ọgwụgwọ, kwado ahụike ọmụmụ na mmekọahụ, ma nyekwa nkwado gbasara ahụike uche.

Ọ bụrụ na onye ahụ nwere nnukwu ọbara ọgbụgba, nnukwu mgbu, ahụ ọkụ, nsogbu ịpụ mmamịrị, ma ọ bụ daa mba, a ga-achọ enyemaka ahụike mberede ozugbo.

Ọ bụrụ na nwa ma ọ bụ onye na-eto eto nọ n’ihe ize ndụ nke FGM, ma ọ bụ mmadụ na-amanye ya ka e mee ya, a ga-akpọtụrụ ọrụ ahụike, safeguarding, child-protection, social-care, ma ọ bụ emergency services ngwa ngwa.

Ibi nwaanyị ugwu, nke a makwaara dị ka female genital mutilation, FGM, nwere ike ibute nsogbu ahụike ozugbo ma ọ bụ n’oge dị anya.

Nsogbu ahụike nwere ike ịgụnye:

nnukwu mgbu na ọbara ọgbụgba;
ọrịa nje;
nsogbu ịpụ mmamịrị;
mmerụ ahụ n’akụkụ anụ ahụ ndị ọzọ;
ọnya siri ike, cyst, ma ọ bụ mgbu na-adịte aka;
ọrịa mmamịrị ma ọ bụ ọrịa n’akụkụ ikpu na-alọghachi ugboro ugboro;
mgbu n’oge ịhụ nsọ;
mgbu n’oge mmekọahụ na nsogbu ndị ọzọ gbasara ahụike mmekọahụ;
nsogbu n’oge ime na ịmụ nwa, dịka ịmụ nwa siri ike, ịdọka anụ ahụ, ma ọ bụ nnukwu ọbara ọgbụgba;
nsogbu uche dịka nchekasị, traumatic stress, mwute, ma ọ bụ ụjọ.

Onye FGM metụtara nwere ike ịchọ enyemaka n’aka dọkịta, nọọsụ, ụlọ ọgwụ ụmụ nwaanyị, sexual-health clinic, maternity service, gynaecology service, mental-health professional, ma ọ bụ specialist FGM service ebe ọrụ ndị a dị. Ha nwere ike nyochaa nsogbu ahụ, nye ọgwụgwọ, kwado ahụike mmekọahụ na ọmụmụ, ma nyekwa nkwado gbasara ahụike uche.

Ọ bụrụ na onye ahụ nwere nnukwu ọbara ọgbụgba, nnukwu mgbu, ahụ ọkụ, nsogbu ịpụ mmamịrị, daa mba, ma ọ bụ nwee nsogbu siri ike ọzọ, a ga-achọ enyemaka ahụike mberede ozugbo.

Ọ bụrụ na nwa agbọghọ nọ n’ihe ize ndụ nke FGM, ma ọ bụ mmadụ na-amanye ma ọ bụ na-eyi ya egwu ka e mee ya, a ga-akpọtụrụ ọrụ ahụike, child-protection, safeguarding, social-care, police, ma ọ bụ emergency services ngwa ngwa.

Onye ọrụ nchekwa ahụike kwesịrị ịkọwa ya n’ụzọ na-asọpụrụ ndị okenye, mana na-ekwukwa eziokwu ahụike nke ọma.

O nwere ike ibido site n’ịghọta na omenala nwere nnukwu uru n’ime obodo, ma kwuo na ebumnuche ya abụghị ịkatọ ndị gara aga ma ọ bụ ịkparị omenala. Mgbe ahụ, o kwesịrị ịkọwa na ihe a na-akpọ ibi ugwu ma ọ bụ FGM bụ ihe ndị otu ahụike mba ụwa na-ahụ dịka omume nwere ike imerụ ahụike ụmụ nwanyị na ụmụ agbọghọ, na enweghị uru ahụike e gosipụtara na ya.

Ọ nwere ike kọwaa nsogbu ndị nwere ike isi na ya pụta, dịka nnukwu mgbu, ọbara ọgbụgba, ọrịa nje, nsogbu ịpụ mmamịrị, ọnya na-adịte aka, mgbu n’oge mmekọahụ, nsogbu n’oge ime na ịmụ nwa, na nsogbu uche.

Ụzọ dị mma isi kparịta ya bụ:

zere okwu dịka “omenala unu dị njọ” ma ọ bụ “ndị na-eme ya amaghị ihe”;
jiri okwu dịka, “Anyị niile chọrọ ka ụmụ agbọghọ anyị too n’ahụike na nsọpụrụ”;
jụọ ndị okenye ihe ha chere omenala ahụ na-echebe ma ọ bụ na-anọchite anya;
kọwaa na a pụrụ ichekwa nsọpụrụ, njirimara omenala na nnabata obodo n’enweghị ịkpụ ahụ nwa agbọghọ;
gwa ha na ọtụtụ usoro ahụike na ikike mmadụ ugbu a na-ele FGM anya dịka omume kwesịrị ịkwụsị n’ihi ihe ize ndụ ọ na-ebute;
ma ọ bụrụ na ọ ga-enyere aka, tinye ndị dọkịta, ndị isi obodo, ndị inyom a na-asọpụrụ, ma ọ bụ ndị isi okpukpe ndị kwadoro ịkwụsị omume ahụ.

Ihe kachasị mkpa bụ ka mkparịta ụka ahụ ghara ịbụ ọgụ megide omenala. O kwesịrị ịbụ mkparịta ụka gbasara otu esi echekwa ahụike, ùgwù na nchekwa ụmụ agbọghọ, ma chọta ụzọ omenala ndị na-adịghị emerụ ahụ.

If someone has been feeling low for a long time, withdrawing from people, and behaving noticeably differently from usual, it may be more than an ordinary difficult period, especially when the changes persist or begin interfering with everyday life.

Signs that professional help would be appropriate include:

low mood, sadness, irritability, or hopelessness lasting around two weeks or longer;
losing interest or pleasure in things they normally enjoy;
withdrawing from family, friends, work, school, or usual activities;
significant changes in sleep, appetite, energy, or concentration;
struggling to manage ordinary responsibilities or personal care;
repeatedly feeling worthless, guilty, helpless, or that things will never improve;
increasing use of alcohol or other substances to cope;
symptoms becoming progressively worse rather than improving.

They do not need to wait until things become severe. A doctor or qualified mental-health professional can assess what is happening, check for other possible causes, and discuss appropriate support such as counselling, psychological therapy, medication, or other care.

If the person talks about wanting to die, harming themselves, feeling that others would be better without them, or appears unable to keep themselves safe, treat this as urgent. Stay with them where possible, reduce access to obvious means of harm, and seek emergency or urgent mental-health care immediately.

Being supportive can also help. Listen without judging or forcing them to “cheer up,” take what they say seriously, and encourage them to get professional help rather than trying to manage persistent symptoms entirely on their own.

If someone has been feeling low for a long time, withdrawing from people, and behaving noticeably differently from usual, it may be more than a temporary bad period, especially when the changes are persistent or interfere with everyday life.

Signs that professional help would be appropriate include:

sadness, emptiness, irritability, or hopelessness lasting around two weeks or longer;
losing interest or pleasure in things they normally enjoy;
increasingly avoiding friends, family, work, school, or other activities;
major changes in sleep, appetite, energy, or concentration;
struggling with normal responsibilities or personal care;
feeling worthless, excessively guilty, or convinced that things will never improve;
using alcohol or drugs more often to cope;
symptoms continuing to worsen rather than gradually improving.

They do not need to wait until things become severe. A doctor or qualified mental-health professional can assess what is happening, consider whether a physical health problem or medication may be contributing, and discuss suitable support or treatment.

If the person says they want to die, harm themselves, or that others would be better off without them, or they cannot keep themselves safe, treat this as urgent. Stay with them if possible and seek emergency or urgent mental-health care immediately.

Family and friends can help by listening without judgement, taking the changes seriously, and encouraging professional support rather than telling the person simply to “cheer up” or cope alone.

If someone has been feeling low for a long time, withdrawing from people, and acting noticeably different from their usual self, it may be more than a temporary bad period, especially if it is affecting everyday life.

Signs that professional help would be appropriate include:

