A family can help by staying calm, supportive, and focused on safety, rather than arguing about what the person is experiencing.

Listen without judging. Let the person describe what they are hearing or feeling.
Do not mock, dismiss, or aggressively challenge the voices. You do not need to agree that the voices are real. You can say that you understand the experience feels real and distressing to them.
Watch for changes in daily functioning, such as stopping eating, sleeping, washing, working, attending school, or communicating with others.
Encourage the person to see a doctor or qualified mental-health professional, especially if the voices are new, becoming more frequent, or the withdrawal is getting worse.
Offer practical help, such as accompanying them to an appointment, helping them keep routines, and reducing stressful or overwhelming situations.
Encourage regular sleep, meals, and avoidance of alcohol or recreational drugs, because these can sometimes worsen symptoms.
Keep communication simple and calm if the person seems frightened, confused, or suspicious.

Seek urgent medical or mental-health help if the person:

talks about harming themselves or someone else;
says the voices are telling them to do something dangerous;
becomes extremely confused, agitated, or unable to care for themselves;
stops eating or drinking;
cannot stay safe;
or develops these symptoms suddenly, particularly alongside fever, head injury, drug use, or another physical illness.

Hearing voices and becoming withdrawn can have several causes, including mental-health conditions, physical illness, medication effects, or substance use. A professional assessment is important to work out what is happening and what support is appropriate.

Family fit support person wey dey hear voices or dey withdraw from people by staying calm, supportive, and focused on safety.

Listen without judging or mocking am. Make the person fit explain wetin e dey experience.
No argue aggressively say the voices no real. You no need agree with wetin the person dey hear. You fit say, “I understand say this thing dey feel real and e dey disturb you.”
Encourage am to see doctor or trained mental-health professional, especially if the voices just start, dey happen more often, or the person dey withdraw more and more.
Help with practical things like going with am to appointment, helping am maintain regular sleep and meals, and reducing stressful situations.
Watch whether the person stop dey eat, drink, sleep, bathe, work, go school, or take care of imself.
Encourage am to avoid alcohol or recreational drugs, because dem fit make some symptoms worse.
If the person dey frightened, confused, or suspicious, talk slowly and calmly and avoid crowding am.

Get urgent medical or mental-health help if the person:

talk say e wan harm imself or another person;
say the voices dey command am to do something dangerous;
become very confused, violent, or unable to care for imself;
stop eating or drinking;
no fit keep imself safe;
or the symptoms start suddenly together with things like fever, head injury, drug use, or serious physical illness.

Hearing voices and withdrawing from people fit happen for different reasons, including mental-health conditions, physical illness, medication effects, or substance use. Professional assessment dey important to know wetin really dey cause am and wetin kind support go help.

Àwọn ará ìdílé lè ṣe àtìlẹ́yìn fún ẹni tí ń gbọ́ àwọn ohùn tàbí tí ó ń ya ara rẹ̀ sọ́tọ̀ sí i nípa jíjẹ́ aláàánú, pẹ̀lẹ́, àti fífi ààbò ẹni náà sí iwájú.

Fetí sí i láì dá a lẹ́bi tàbí fi ẹ̀gàn kàn án. Jẹ́ kí ó lè sọ ohun tí ó ń gbọ́ tàbí ohun tí ó ń ní ìrírí.
Má ṣe bá a jiyàn gidigidi pé ohun tí ó ń gbọ́ “kò sí.” Ẹ kò nílò láti fara mọ́ ohun tí ó sọ pé ó ń gbọ́. Ẹ lè sọ pé, “Mo mọ̀ pé ohun tí o ń ní ìrírí yìí ń yọ ọ lẹ́nu gan-an.”
Gba a níyànjú láti rí dókítà tàbí onímọ̀ ìlera ọpọlọ, pàápàá bí àwọn ohùn náà bá ṣẹ̀ṣẹ̀ bẹ̀rẹ̀, bá ń pọ̀ sí i, tàbí bí ó ṣe ń ya ara rẹ̀ sọ́tọ̀ bá ń buru sí i.
Ran án lọ́wọ́ pẹ̀lú àwọn ohun ojoojúmọ́, bí lílọ sí appointment, jíjẹ oúnjẹ déédéé, oorun tó pé, àti dídín wahalà kù.
Ṣọ́ra fún ayípadà bí ẹni náà bá dáwọ́ jíjẹ, mímu omi, oorun, wíwẹ̀, iṣẹ́, ilé-ẹ̀kọ́, tàbí ìtọ́jú ara rẹ̀ dúró.
Gba a níyànjú láti yẹra fún ọtí tàbí recreational drugs, nítorí wọ́n lè mú àwọn àmì kan buru sí i.
Bí ẹni náà bá ń bẹ̀rù, dàrú, tàbí fura sí gbogbo ènìyàn, bá a sọ̀rọ̀ ní pẹ̀lẹ́ àti ní ọ̀rọ̀ tó rọrùn.

Ẹ yẹ kí ẹ wá ìrànlọ́wọ́ pajawiri bí ẹni náà:

bá sọ pé ó fẹ́ ṣe ara rẹ̀ tàbí ẹlòmíràn nípalára;
bá sọ pé àwọn ohùn ń pàṣẹ fún un láti ṣe ohun tó léwu;
bá dàrú gidigidi tàbí kò lè tọ́jú ara rẹ̀;
bá dáwọ́ jíjẹ tàbí mímu omi dúró;
bá kò lè dá ara rẹ̀ láàbò;
tàbí bí àwọn àmì náà bá bẹ̀rẹ̀ lojijì pẹ̀lú ibà, ìfarapa orí, lílo oògùn/drugs, tàbí àìsàn ara tó le.

Ìgbọ́ ohùn àti yíyà ara ẹni sọ́tọ̀ lè ní ọ̀pọ̀ ìdí. Àyẹ̀wò lọ́dọ̀ onímọ̀ ìlera ṣe pàtàkì láti mọ ohun tó ń fa a àti irú ìtọ́jú tó yẹ.

Ezinụlọ nwere ike inyere onye na-anụ ihe ndị ọzọ anaghị anụ, ma ọ bụ onye na-anọpụ iche karịa ka ọ na-adị, site n’ịdị jụụ, na-akwado ya, ma na-elekwasị anya na nchekwa ya.

Gee ya ntị n’enweghị ikpe ma ọ bụ ịkwa emo. Nye ya ohere ịkọ ihe ọ na-anụ ma ọ bụ otú ọ na-adị ya.
Ekwesịghị ịlụ ọgụ na ya banyere ma ihe ọ na-anụ “dị adị” ma ọ bụ na ọ dịghị. Ị nwere ike ikwu na ị ghọtara na ahụmahụ ahụ na-adị ya ezigbo njọ ma ọ bụ na-atụ ya ụjọ.
Gbaa ya ume ka ọ hụ dọkịta ma ọ bụ ọkachamara ahụike uche, karịsịa ma ọ bụrụ na ihe ndị a bụ ihe ọhụrụ, na-akawanye njọ, ma ọ bụ na-eme ka ọ kwụsị mmekọrịta na ndị ọzọ.
Nyere ya aka n’ihe ndị dị mfe, dịka iso ya gaa n’ụlọ ọgwụ, inyere ya aka iri nri nke ọma, hie ụra nke ọma, ma belata nrụgide.
Lezie anya ma onye ahụ akwụsịla iri nri, ịṅụ mmiri, ịsa ahụ, ịga ọrụ ma ọ bụ ụlọ akwụkwọ, ma ọ bụ ilekọta onwe ya.
Gbaa ya ume ka ọ zere mmanya na ọgwụ ndị mmadụ ji eme ihe n’ụzọ ntụrụndụ, n’ihi na ụfọdụ n’ime ha nwere ike ime ka mgbaàmà ka njọ.
Ọ bụrụ na ọ na-atụ ụjọ, na-enwe mgbagwoju anya, ma ọ bụ na-enyo ndị mmadụ enyo nke ukwuu, gwa ya okwu nwayọọ na n'ụzọ dị mfe.

Chọọ enyemaka ahụike mberede ma ọ bụrụ na onye ahụ:

kwuru na ọ chọrọ imerụ onwe ya ma ọ bụ onye ọzọ ahụ;
kwuru na olu ọ na-anụ na-agwa ya ka o mee ihe dị ize ndụ;
na-enwe mgbagwoju anya nke ukwuu ma ọ bụ enweghị ike ilekọta onwe ya;
akwụsị iri nri ma ọ bụ ịṅụ mmiri;
enweghị ike idobe onwe ya n’ebe nchekwa;
ma ọ bụ mgbaàmà ndị ahụ malitere na mberede tinyere ahụ ọkụ, mmerụ isi, iji ọgwụ/drugs, ma ọ bụ ọrịa anụ ahụ siri ike.

Ịnụ olu na ịnọpụ iche nwere ike inwe ọtụtụ ihe kpatara ya. Nnyocha n’aka dọkịta ma ọ bụ ọkachamara ahụike uche dị mkpa iji mata ihe na-akpata ya na ụdị enyemaka kwesịrị ekwesị.

