kí ó sọ gbogbo àwọn àmì tó ní fún oníṣègùn;
kí ó má ṣe bẹ̀rẹ̀ oògùn malaria nítorí pé malaria wọ́pọ̀ ní àgbègbè náà nìkan;
kí ó gba ìtọ́jú ní kíákíá bí àìlera bá le, bí ìmí bá nira, bí eebi bá ń ṣẹlẹ̀ léraléra, tàbí bí ìdàrúdàpọ̀ tàbí pípàdánù ìmọ̀ bá ṣẹlẹ̀.

Ní ṣókí, àyẹ̀wò ló yẹ kí ó ṣáájú ìtọ́jú, nítorí àwọn àmì nìkan kò tó láti fi dá malaria mọ́.

They should stop repeatedly treating the fever as malaria without confirmation and get medically assessed and tested.

A fever that keeps returning can have several causes. Malaria is one possibility, but repeated treatment without testing can delay finding the actual cause.

They should:

go to a clinic or health facility for a malaria test and clinical assessment;
tell the health worker what medicines they have already taken and how often;
avoid starting another course of malaria medicine without appropriate advice;
seek urgent care if there is confusion, seizures, difficulty breathing, repeated vomiting, severe weakness, inability to drink, or loss of consciousness.

The key point is: recurrent fever after repeated unconfirmed malaria treatment needs reassessment, not another automatic malaria treatment.

